# Mutation cases for "the detector's reach is derived, and the door it cannot
# walk is measured shut rather than assumed shut".
#
# Run with
# `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/the_detectors_reach_is_derived_not_named.cases`.
#
# The round before this one taught `workflow_trigger_coverage` to follow a read
# through `sce-build/tests/common`, and registered two holes it had MEASURED
# nothing about: the walk started at a path spelled into the source, and a test
# can also read past a filter by calling a library it links. Both were left
# open on purpose — a prescription written over an unmeasured population is the
# thing this gate exists to refuse.
#
# Measured 2026-09-03, before either was touched:
#   * file-backed modules declared by `sce-build/tests/*.rs`: `common` and
#     nothing else, across 17 declaring sources; `#[path]` attributes: 0.
#   * the two signatures across the libraries these targets link — this
#     crate's 96 sources plus the 34 in the two crates its manifest binds by
#     path: 0 occurrences, raw text, comments included.
#
# So the two halves get different treatments, and the difference is the point:
#
#   * The module walk has a REAL population, so its answer is derived — roots
#     from what the test sources declare, children from what each module file
#     declares, and each directory cross-checked against its own contents.
#     Every part of that can be shown red, and the cases below do it.
#   * The library door has an EMPTY one. Attribution over an empty population
#     measures nothing and no mutation can redden it, so it is a tripwire
#     instead: put a signature in any of the 130 files and the sweep says so.
#     ⚠ The repair when it fires is to bring that crate into the walk — not to
#     delete the read, and not to add the file to an exemption list.
#
# ⚠ The sweep reads RAW text on purpose. Stripping prose removes characters
# and never adds them, so a signature the code spells is a signature the file
# spells: the scan can over-report and cannot under-report. That is the safe
# direction for a tripwire, and it is why the last case below works by adding
# a comment.

mutation_tests -p sce-build --features cli --test workflow_trigger_coverage
mutation_targets sce-build/tests/workflow_trigger_coverage.rs
# Read at run time: the manifest is the declaration the library sweep derives
# its population from, and lib.rs is one of the files it examines.
mutation_runtime_targets sce-build/Cargo.toml sce-build/src/lib.rs

# ── The roots are what the tests declare ───────────────────────────
#
# The defect this half repairs is a path spelled into the source. It cannot be
# restored directly — `tests/common` is still today's only answer, so naming it
# gives the same set — which is exactly why the cases attack the READING
# instead. A reader that stops recognising a declaration returns no roots at
# all, and the floor refuses that rather than reporting an empty module.

mutation_case "a module declaration stops being recognised" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/workflow_trigger_coverage.rs",
     '''            let rest = t
                .strip_prefix("pub mod ")
                .or_else(|| t.strip_prefix("pub(crate) mod "))
                .or_else(|| t.strip_prefix("mod "))?;''',
     '''            let rest = t
                .strip_prefix("pub module ")
                .or_else(|| t.strip_prefix("pub(crate) module "))
                .or_else(|| t.strip_prefix("module "))?;''')
PY

# ── A module file's children live where the language says ──────────
#
# A `mod.rs` owns its own directory; any other module file owns one named
# after it. Losing the first arm sends the walk to `tests/common/mod/`, and a
# declaration that resolves to nothing is a hard error rather than a smaller
# module — the whole point of the resolver being written out.

mutation_case "a mod.rs is treated as an ordinary module file" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/workflow_trigger_coverage.rs",
     '''    if is_crate_root || path.file_name().and_then(|n| n.to_str()) == Some("mod.rs") {''',
     '''    if is_crate_root {''')
PY

# The other spelling, from the other side. `common` is a directory today, so
# a resolver that knows only `NAME.rs` finds nothing.

mutation_case "only the flat spelling of a module file is looked for" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/workflow_trigger_coverage.rs",
     '''    let nested = dir.join(name).join("mod.rs");''',
     '''    let nested = dir.join(name).join("no-such-file.rs");''')
PY

# ── The walk goes past the roots ───────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ NOT a case here. Stopping at the root — reading `common/mod.rs` and none
# of the four files it declares — is the subject
# `a_read_through_the_shared_module_is_still_a_read.cases` already owns, under
# "the module is read as its root file alone". That case was written against
# the older shape of this function and was re-anchored to the walk in the same
# round as this file, rather than copied here: two casefiles reddening on one
# mutation would report a subject as doubly covered while nothing new is being
# measured.
#
# What DOES belong here is the cross-check, because it is that case's only
# witness — no assertion about workflows moves when the walk stops short.

mutation_case "a file in the module directory is not missed" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/workflow_trigger_coverage.rs",
     '''            .filter(|p| !seen.contains(*p))
            .map(|p| p.display().to_string())''',
     '''            .filter(|p| seen.contains(*p))
            .map(|p| p.display().to_string())''')
PY

# ── The library door: the population comes from the manifest ───────
#
# A crate the scan recognises and then does not collect is a library this
# suite links and never examines. The equality against what the scan COUNTED
# is what says so — a floor written as a number would have to be edited every
# time the manifest grows, and would then be the thing that is stale.
#
# The collection is put behind a branch that cannot be taken rather than
# deleted: removing the only `push` leaves the accumulator's mutability
# unnecessary, `-D warnings` refuses the tree, and the case reports
# INCONCLUSIVE instead of a red. That is how this one first came back.

mutation_case "a bound path dependency is counted and not collected" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/workflow_trigger_coverage.rs",
     '''        roots.push(crate_dir.join("src"));''',
     '''        if !crate_dir.is_dir() {
            roots.push(crate_dir.join("src"));
        }''')
PY

mutation_case "the manifest scan stops recognising a dependency table" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/workflow_trigger_coverage.rs",
     '''        if name.trim().is_empty() || !rest.trim_start().starts_with('{') {''',
     '''        if name.trim().is_empty() || !rest.trim_start().starts_with('[') {''')
PY

# ── And the door itself, opened ────────────────────────────────────
#
# This is the case the tripwire exists for, and the only one that proves it is
# a tripwire rather than a comment. A library gains a mention of the workflow
# directory; a test calling into that crate would inherit the input set, and
# the module walk above does not reach there.

mutation_case "a library this suite links starts naming the workflow tree" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/src/lib.rs",
     "// sce-build — SCXML code generator for sce-rust-runtime.",
     "// sce-build — SCXML code generator for sce-rust-runtime.\n"
     "// Lane list read from .github/workflows at build time.")
PY

# Two mutations deliberately NOT written, for two different reasons:
#
#   * Removing the `#[path]` refusal cannot be observed. No Rust source this
#     walk opens carries one, measured today, so the guard is unreachable from
#     the real tree and a case for it would report CAUGHT off some other row.
#     It stays because an attribute that moves a module somewhere the resolver
#     does not look is unclassified rather than absent — and rule (6) says an
#     unclassified thing is red, not a pass.
#   * Naming `tests/common` back into the source is a no-op. It is still the
#     only module the tests declare, so the named answer and the derived one
#     agree today. A case that cannot fail is not evidence, and the derivation
#     is held instead by the four reading cases above.
